    "_ulid": "01KJDQ1DQMCBX32KDVWBJBF8W1",
    "slugs": [],
    "title": "ULID short ref ambiguity in batch operations: when two ULIDs share a prefix (e.g. 01KJ4SNE9 and 01KJ",
    "type": "task",
    "description": "ULID short ref ambiguity in batch operations: when two ULIDs share a prefix (e.g. 01KJ4SNE9 and 01KJ4SNEC), short refs like @01KJ4SNE fail. kspec already has a shortest-unique-ID utility — this should be used anywhere refs are displayed (triage list, task list, inbox list, promote result_ref, etc.) so that presented refs are always unambiguous and copy-pasteable into subsequent commands.",
    "spec_ref": "@batch-exec",
    "status": "pending",
    "blocked_by": [],
    "depends_on": [],
    "context": [],
    "priority": 3,
    "tags": [],
    "vcs_refs": [],
    "created_at": "2026-02-26T19:33:01.300Z",
    "notes": [
      {
        "_ulid": "01KJDQA0MV956KKZREMG980AE6",
        "created_at": "2026-02-26T19:37:42.812Z",
        "author": "@claude",
        "content": "ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\n\n**What**: When two ULIDs share a prefix (e.g., 01KJ4SNE9... and 01KJ4SNEC...), the default 8-character short ref `@01KJ4SNE` is ambiguous and fails to resolve. The `ReferenceIndex.shortUlid()` method (in `src/parser/refs.ts` line 310) already computes the minimum unique prefix, but many places in the codebase use a hardcoded `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` instead. There are 69 occurrences of `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` across 19 files.\n\n**Where**: All places that format ULID refs for display need to use `shortUlid()` instead of `_ulid.slice(0, 8)`. Key files with hardcoded slicing:\n- `src/cli/commands/triage.ts` (14 occurrences) — triage list, record, act, get, start\n- `src/cli/commands/inbox.ts` (9 occurrences) — inbox list, add, promote\n- `src/cli/commands/item.ts` (6 occurrences) — item list, get, add\n- `src/cli/commands/plan.ts` (9 occurrences) — plan list, get\n- `src/cli/commands/search.ts` (4 occurrences) — search results\n- `src/cli/commands/session/context.ts` (4 occurrences) — session context display\n- `src/triage/actions.ts` (1 occurrence) — promote result ref\n- `src/parser/validate.ts` (8 occurrences) — validation output\n- Several other files with 1-3 occurrences each\n\nThe `ReferenceIndex` is already available in most of these contexts (it's built during `initContext()` or `buildIndexes()`).\n\n**How**:\n1. For each file, replace `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` with `index.shortUlid(_ulid)` where a ReferenceIndex is in scope\n2. For files where no index is available, either thread the index through or create a lightweight shortening utility\n3. For triage records (which aren't indexed in the main ReferenceIndex), consider building a local index or a dedicated triage ULID shortener\n4. Ensure that refs displayed by any list/get/result command are always copy-pasteable into subsequent commands without ambiguity\n\n**Done when**:\n- No occurrences of `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` remain in display-facing code paths\n- All displayed refs (triage list, task list, inbox list, search results, promote result_ref, etc.) use `shortUlid()` or equivalent\n- A test with two items sharing an 8-character ULID prefix shows that displayed refs are still unique and resolvable",
        "supersedes": null
      }
    ],
    "todos": [],
    "automation": "eligible",
    "_sourceFile": "/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml"
  },
  {
    "_ulid": "01KJDQ1EKVV4V60V7WT6DCK3H3",
    "slugs": [],
    "title": "Triage promote action should optionally delete the inbox item after creating the task. Currently pro",
    "type": "task",
    "description": "Triage promote action should optionally delete the inbox item after creating the task. Currently promoted items stay in inbox requiring manual cleanup. Consider --consume flag or making deletion the default for promote (with snapshot preserved in triage record).",
    "spec_ref": "@triage-cli-commands",
    "status": "pending",
    "blocked_by": [],
    "depends_on": [],
    "context": [],
    "priority": 3,
    "tags": [],
    "vcs_refs": [],
    "created_at": "2026-02-26T19:33:02.203Z",
    "notes": [
      {
        "_ulid": "01KJDQAHM5SX18W9F5V0ET02G6",
        "created_at": "2026-02-26T19:38:00.197Z",
        "author": "@claude",
        "content": "ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\n\n**What**: When `kspec triage act` executes a \"promote\" action, it creates a new task from the inbox item but leaves the original inbox item in the inbox. This requires a separate manual `kspec inbox delete` step to clean up, which is tedious and easy to forget. The inbox item's text is already preserved in the triage record's `item_snapshot` field, so the original is not needed for audit trail purposes.\n\n**Where**: \n- `src/triage/actions.ts` lines 54-74 — the `promote` case in `executeTriageAction()`. Currently creates the task and returns, but never calls `deleteInboxItem()` for the promoted item.\n- `src/cli/commands/triage.ts` lines 235-289 — the `triage act` command handler. This is where the `--consume` flag would be parsed and passed to `executeTriageAction()`.\n- The `delete` and `duplicate` cases (lines 77-90, 117-130) already call `deleteInboxItem()` — the promote case can follow the same pattern.\n\n**How**: Two reasonable approaches:\n1. **Flag approach**: Add `--consume` flag to `kspec triage act` that deletes the inbox item after promote. Pass it through `ExecuteTriageActionOptions` to `executeTriageAction()`.\n2. **Default behavior**: Make deletion the default for promote (since the snapshot is preserved in the triage record), with a `--keep` flag to opt out.\n\nRecommendation: Option 2 (delete by default) is cleaner since every promoted item has its text preserved in `item_snapshot`. The `--keep` flag is the escape hatch. The `delete` and `duplicate` cases already delete inbox items as their default behavior, so promote doing the same is consistent.\n\nImplementation:\n1. Add `consume` option to `ExecuteTriageActionOptions` (default true for promote)\n2. In the `promote` case of `executeTriageAction()`, after creating the task, call `deleteInboxItem()` for `record.inbox_ref` unless consume is false\n3. Add `--keep` flag to `kspec triage act` command that sets `consume: false`\n4. Update dry-run output to mention deletion\n\n**Done when**:\n- `kspec triage act <ref>` on a promote record creates the task AND deletes the inbox item\n- `kspec triage act <ref> --keep` creates the task but preserves the inbox item\n- Dry-run output mentions that the inbox item would be deleted\n- The `item_snapshot` in the triage record still contains the original text for audit trail\n- Tests cover: promote with deletion (default), promote with --keep, dry-run output",
        "supersedes": null
      }
    ],
    "todos": [],
    "automation": "eligible",
    "_sourceFile": "/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml"
  },
